1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hakon_Ahlberg '''Hakon Ahlberg'''] (June 10, 1891, Harplinge – March 12, 1984, Stockholm) was a Swedish architect, editor and author, best known as the official architect for the repa
2 CONC ir and restoration of Gripsholm Castle near the town of Mariefred in central Sweden. He was one of the founding members and first president of SAR, the Swedish Architects’ Association, and was well known as an active participant i
2 CONC n architectural debate in Sweden, and was editor-in-chief of the Swedish architectural journals Arkitekten in 1922 and Byggmästaren 1922–24. He was also president of the Swedish Academy of Fine Arts 1954–62.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahlberg studied architecture at the Royal Institute of Technology in Stockholm, completing his studies in 1914. He also undertook studies at the Royal Swedish Academy of Arts in 1914–1917, one of his most influential teachers bein
2 CONC g Ivar Tengbom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahlberg's principal role in Swedish architecture was not as a “leader of style” but as an idealistic leader, as an organiser and spokesman of the Swedish architectural profession. He was president of the Swedish Association of Arc
2 CONC hitects.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an architect he was part of the brief movement called Nordic Classicism, but with clear links with Swedish vernacular architecture, as expressed in the simple wooden houses he designed for the Swedish trade union-run folk high 
2 CONC school in Brunnsvik, Ludvika parish in 1928. He designed a number of churches (e.g. Mälarhöjden Church, Stockholm, 1929), museums, and hospitals as well as housing. His most important works are the Arts and Crafts pavilion at the 
2 CONC Gothenburg exhibition in 1923, and the PUB department store in central Stockholm from 1924. Ahlberg designed a number of hospitals, including the Sidsjön mental hospital in Sundsvall 1939–44, the Children's clinic in Oslo, Norway 
2 CONC (1946–50), and the University Hospital in Maracaibo in Venezuela (1946–54).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1957 Ahlberg was awarded the Prince Eugen Medal and in 1973 the Alvar Aalto Medal.
